00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:26.630
This delighted me because I just imagined Jen Googling company picnics.

255
00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:28.430
She was a lady who Googled.

256
00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:30.750
I was going to say, like, that would have been us.

257
00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:33.730
We would have been like, wait, let's research what people do.

258
00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.910
And one of the things that came up was a volleyball tournament.

259
00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:38.610
So they thought that would be fun.

260
00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:43.110
And Paul said he thought it was a great way to bring everyone back together, all the different

261
00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:47.110
branches, all the different characters for this big 100th episode.

262
00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:52.490
The only characters you don't see that were in the world of the show really were Jan.

263
00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:54.650
But it doesn't make sense for Jan to come back.

264
00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:55.070
Yes.

265
00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:55.830
And Karen.

266
00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:57.650
Yes, we got mail about that.

267
00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:58.530
Where was Karen?

268
00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:05.970
And Jen Salata did an interview where she said Rashida Jones was filming Parks and Recreation

269
00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.510
and they couldn't get her for a whole week of work.

270
00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:09.750
to be at this picnic.

271
00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:14.070
And then they realized, wait, she would be on maternity leave.

272
00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:18.950
So they tracked her story-wise as being on maternity leave and that's why she wasn't

273
00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:19.150
there.

274
00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:20.610
Well, that worked out perfect.

275
00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:25.910
While the volleyball was a great idea, Ken did share that it was a logistical nightmare

276
00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:30.630
because they started filming and they were trying to keep with the rules of volleyball.

277
00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:31.050
Okay.

278
00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:36.690
Which means the teams trade sides depending on score or whatever, but they just had to bail.

279
00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:39.750
They had to bail because it just got too complicated.

280
00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:45.250
And Jen said she had this memory of looking over at Ken and he had a clipboard and he

281
00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:51.910
had a little quarter and he was tracing this coin with a pencil and placing it and mapping

282
00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:56.670
out where everyone was going to stand and who was serving and who was playing what position

283
00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:59.590
because it was so complex to shoot.

284
00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:03.830
I thought it was interesting that they chose volleyball.

285
00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:07.410
Any company picnic I've ever been to, it's usually softball.

286
00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:11.550
But I do see how that people are really spread out in softball.

287
00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:13.310
So I guess that makes sense to me.

288
00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:13.770
Right.

289
00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:15.790
But I mean, I can't even imagine.

290
00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:17.590
Jen and Paul shared this other thing.

291
00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:22.910
Jen said that her and Paul, as they wrote this episode, they thought that each team had

292
00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:23.670
nine players.

293
00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:27.830
And so they wrote the whole script with each team having nine players.

294
00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:28.470
Oh, no.

295
00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.190
And then they got to the production meeting.

296
00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:34.970
And in the production meeting, someone was like, you know, on volleyball teams, you have

297
00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.290
six players on the court, not nine.

298
00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:38.690
And Paul and them were like, what?

299
00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:41.950
Gosh.

300
00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:44.430
But yeah, so they had to change that like immediately.

301
00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:48.550
And I have more fun tidbits from the three of them in the commentary I'll sprinkle through

302
00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:49.050
the episode.

303
00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:49.870
All right.

304
00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:50.550
Angela.

305
00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:51.030
What?

306
00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:52.370
Great job on that fast fact.

307
00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:53.270
Oh, thank you.

308
00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:54.110
I really enjoyed that.

309
00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:55.250
Thanks so much.

310
00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:55.730
You told me.

311
00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:59.370
You were like, Jenna, there's some great stuff about the inspiration behind the episode.

312
00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:02.610
And I said, I'm holding a spot for you up in fast facts, lady.

313
00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:03.110
You did.

314
00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:04.210
All right.

315
00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:08.490
Fast fact number two is a location breakdown.

316
00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:15.170
Now, the signs hanging in this episode say that this picnic is being held at the Meadows

317
00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:16.670
in upstate New York.

318
00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:21.490
I could not find any information on this or if this is a real place.

319
00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:21.950
Hmm.

320
00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:23.210
Fakie place.

321
00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:24.170
Perhaps.

322
00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:25.250
Does anyone know?

323
00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:31.190
Please comment on Office Ladies Pod if you've ever been to a park called the Meadows in upstate

324
00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:31.570
New York.

325
00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:32.270
I'd love to know.

326
00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:33.550
Fan question.

327
00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:39.410
Rayyan from Pakistan would like to know, where did we actually film this episode?

328
00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:43.370
Well, we filmed at Kalamigos Ranch in Malibu Canyon.

329
00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:46.310
It is a beautiful location.

330
00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:51.150
It's kind of nestled into the side of this canyon in the Malibu Mountains.

331
00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:56.930
Did you know it has its own water source from a spring on the property?

332
00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:00.410
I didn't know that, but I know a lot of people get married there.

333
00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:01.990
I've been to a wedding there.

334
00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:03.010
You know what?

335
00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:08.170
Randy Cordray knew about this location because he had been the best man at a wedding there.

336
00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:13.090
And when the script came up, he remembered it and suggested it to our locations manager,

337
00:15:13.270 --> 00:15:13.990
Kyle Alexander.

338
00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:17.270
And they scouted it, and they all agreed it was perfect.

339
00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:22.690
So now, Angela, you mentioned how Ken Quapas said, like, this was a logistically difficult

340
00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:23.330
shoot.

341
00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:23.830
Mm-hmm.

342
00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:29.990
Randy said this was a very difficult episode to pull off logistically from a production side

343
00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:32.510
because we had so many people.

344
00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:35.350
So many people, and we were in the middle of a canyon.

345
00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:39.910
He said we had to hire additional crew, including multiple medics.

346
00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:40.550
Mm.

347
00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:44.570
And we had hydration stations and sunscreen stations.

348
00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.470
And Sergio and Alan are caterers.

349
00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:49.290
They had to triple their staff.

350
00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:53.770
They had three lines to serve lunch, like three buffet lines.

351
00:15:53.770 --> 00:16:00.850
And then, Peter and Vartan at craft service, they also had three stations instead of one.

352
00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:06.870
It was a lot of concern about being able to feed everybody because all the background food,

353
00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:08.690
people were told not to eat.

354
00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:10.010
Yeah, it was fakie food.

355
00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:16.050
It was real food, but it had been at various temperatures that made it sort of unsafe to eat.

356
00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:21.810
So if you watch the episode, you might notice no one eats anything off of their plates of food.

357
00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:22.390
Hmm.

358
00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:23.170
Check it out.

359
00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:26.490
Ken said there was probably like 250 people there.

360
00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:29.230
Guess what my fast fact number three is, lady?

361
00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:29.710
What?

362
00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:31.170
I'll tell you.

363
00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:39.750
We had 14 guest actors, our regular cast, and 185 background performers for this episode.

364
00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:41.950
Also, I thought this was interesting.

365
00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:48.330
Some of the background performers were called special business performers.

366
00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:49.450
Special business?

367
00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:51.450
You know what their special business was?

368
00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:52.390
Volleyball?

369
00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:52.970
Yeah.

370
00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.330
Playing volleyball for hours on end.

371
00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:58.110
Lady, were you ever a background performer?

372
00:16:58.450 --> 00:16:59.210
You know it.

373
00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:00.710
That's how I earned my SAG card.

374
00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:01.790
Yeah, same.

375
00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:06.010
Did you have any special business listed on your background performer resume?

376
00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:07.190
Yes, I did.

377
00:17:07.370 --> 00:17:08.670
What was your special business?

378
00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:10.310
That I could speak Indonesian.

379
00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:11.430
Oh.

380
00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:12.070
Mm-hmm.

381
00:17:12.070 --> 00:17:18.450
My special business was water skiing, bowling, and animal handling.

382
00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:21.890
Oh, I also put I could play tennis and roller skate.

383
00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:22.170
Yes.

384
00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:23.010
Those are a big deal.

385
00:17:23.090 --> 00:17:24.090
Yeah, that's a big deal.

386
00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.070
I was on 90210.

387
00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:28.430
Did you have to do any of your special business?

388
00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:29.250
I didn't.

389
00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:35.410
I was one of Tori Spelling's sorority sisters, but I did pass the ballots of a voting, a sorority

390
00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:40.010
voting session to, I handed them to Tori.

391
00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.250
Oh, you got to interact.

392
00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:43.390
Yes, briefly.

393
00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:44.230
I said nothing.

394
00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:45.730
I just handed some paper to her.

395
00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:51.150
I was an extra on the movie Pleasantville, starring Reese Witherspoon and Tobey Maguire.

396
00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:53.270
And I had to play a teenager.

397
00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:57.130
And that was really fun because I got to dress up in old-timey clothes the whole time.

398
00:17:57.790 --> 00:17:59.250
But check this out.

399
00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:06.450
They came into the extras holding area because they needed three couples to make out in the

400
00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:07.310
backseat of a car.

401
00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:12.050
They were going to, like, pan across all these different couples making out until they got

402
00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:13.690
to the car with the lead actors.

403
00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:15.470
Were you part of the make out couples?

404
00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:15.870
No.

405
00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:20.210
You would have become a featured extra if you did it and you got a bump in pay, but I felt

406
00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:21.810
too shy, so I didn't volunteer.

407
00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:29.470
But lady, we had a very special background performer on this episode of The Office, and

408
00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:34.050
it was Jenny Tan who runs the fan website officetelly.com.

409
00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:34.910
Yes.

410
00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:37.030
We had all become friends with her.

411
00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:43.090
And she was invited to spend the entire week with us, getting an exclusive look at the finale,

412
00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:45.210
and they asked her to be an extra.

413
00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:52.130
And lady, she documented all five days of shooting this episode on officetelly.com.

414
00:18:52.250 --> 00:18:55.770
It is still there, and she has tons of photos.

415
00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:57.010
Amazing.

416
00:18:57.530 --> 00:19:03.350
She also gave this amazing breakdown of what it was like to be a background performer on

417
00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:04.090
Company Picnic.

418
00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:05.910
Oh my gosh, are you going to share?

419
00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:06.470
Yes.

420
00:19:06.850 --> 00:19:12.750
You guys, Jenny was sort of someone who made a community for The Office online before anyone

421
00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:13.570
was doing that.

422
00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:14.690
Before social media.

423
00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:15.890
Before social media.

424
00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:21.570
And she was there for the show, providing this community in our earliest days, when we

425
00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:23.150
didn't even know the future of the show.

426
00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:26.110
And it meant a lot to all of us.

427
00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:32.050
And we would reach out to her and give her exclusive tips or content or photos that she

428
00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:34.490
would post on the website, and it became kind of the hub.

429
00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:35.210
Yeah.

430
00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:41.170
And this was before networks did any kind of social media or website for any of their shows.

431
00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:43.910
And our show had this presence because of Jenny.

432
00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:51.390
And so her documenting of our show is very extensive, and it's all still there on officetelly.com.

433
00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:52.850
There's all these exclusive interviews.

434
00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:53.550
It's amazing.

435
00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:58.230
She said you could really tell the people who were professional background artists because

436
00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:05.690
they would bring tons of gear, like rolling luggage filled with what she said, quote, outerwear

437
00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:12.290
for every possible weather condition, chairs, water, snacks, something to read.

438
00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:17.070
It's like, you know, when you go to the beach and you see people like come with the wagon

439
00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:21.450
full of stuff or at the soccer game, that's a professional background performer.

440
00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:22.090
Oh, yeah.

441
00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:26.890
I remember doing background work, and there was a guy that had a backpack that was a chair.

442
00:20:27.190 --> 00:20:27.630
Yes.

443
00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:31.610
And you could open the backpack and there was stuff inside, or you could unfold it and sit

444
00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:31.810
in it.

445
00:20:31.910 --> 00:20:34.150
Because background performers do not get trailers.

446
00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:39.270
You mostly are going to be directed to a holding area with some folding chairs in it.

447
00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:42.410
But my experience was there were never enough chairs for each person.

448
00:20:42.590 --> 00:20:44.570
Never enough chairs, and they're usually outside.

449
00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:45.210
Yeah.

450
00:20:45.730 --> 00:20:50.230
So Jenny said that when she first went to the wardrobe tent, she was given an apron to wear,

451
00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:52.030
and she said she would be a grill cook.

452
00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:57.670
But then about an hour later, production pulled her aside and made her a Buffalo branch employee.

453
00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:01.750
And then they winked and said, you're going to be part of a special branch.

454
00:21:02.030 --> 00:21:03.710
But she didn't know what that meant yet.

455
00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:04.030
Oh.

456
00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:05.370
And get this.

457
00:21:05.750 --> 00:21:11.970
Jenny talked to a bunch of the background folks, and she said that most of them were not

458
00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:17.130
office fans, that they had never seen the show, and that a few of them did not know who Steve

459
00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:17.790
Carell was.

460
00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:24.110
She said they were thrilled to be working on our show because we had a reputation for treating

461
00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:27.890
background performers very, very well and for providing great food.

462
00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:34.030
She said one person told her from a work perspective, to be able to be an extra on the office for an

463
00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:36.250
entire week, I feel like I won the lottery.

464
00:21:36.510 --> 00:21:39.310
So that made me really proud that our show did a good job.

465
00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:45.090
Because as a person who did a lot of background work, it meant a lot.

466
00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:49.970
Usually the background performers were like the last people who get to go through the lunch line,

467
00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.330
and it's like what's left, almost nothing.

468
00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:54.970
And you were not allowed to go to craft services.

469
00:21:55.350 --> 00:22:02.350
But on our show, we had a whole buffet set aside just for the background performers and a whole craft

470
00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:04.930
services table that they kept full.

471
00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:06.190
And it was awesome.

472
00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:08.190
Well, you know, that's Randy Cordray.

473
00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:10.730
Randy took care of everyone.

474
00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:14.910
However you were a part of our show, he was going to make sure you were taken care of.

475
00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:16.670
That is absolutely true, lady.

476
00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:18.050
All right, lady.

477
00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:19.490
Those are all my fast facts.

478
00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:20.410
I mean, it's a lot.

479
00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:22.110
Well, it's a huge episode.

480
00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:22.670
I know.

481
00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:23.690
All right.

482
00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:27.730
Should we take a break and then start breaking down this episode?

483
00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:28.630
Just heads up.

484
00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:29.610
It's going to be a long one.

